1039→\section{Proposition Proofs}
1040→\label{app:proofs}
1041→
1042→\textbf{Proof of Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}} (Routing Information Loss Under RevIN).
1043→
1044→\begin{proof}
1045→\emph{Part (i)}: The RevIN map $N: \mathbf{x} \mapsto \mathbf{S} = (\mathbf{x} - M)/\Sigma$ is deterministic, so for any router that operates on $\mathbf{S}$ we have the Markov chain $X \to S \to E$. The Data Processing Inequality gives $I(X;E) \geq I(S;E)$.
1046→
1047→\emph{Part (ii)}: By the chain rule of mutual information,
1048→\begin{align*}
1049→I(X; E) &= I(M, \Sigma, S;\, E) = I(S;\, E) + I(M, \Sigma;\, E \mid S).
1050→\end{align*}
1051→Under the assumption $(M, \Sigma) \perp\!\!\!\perp S$, the conditional mutual information simplifies:
1052→\begin{align*}
1053→I(M, \Sigma;\, E \mid S) &= H(M, \Sigma \mid S) - H(M, \Sigma \mid E, S) \\
1054→&= H(M, \Sigma) - H(M, \Sigma \mid E, S) = I(M, \Sigma;\, E, S) \\
1055→&= I(M, \Sigma;\, E) + I(M, \Sigma;\, S \mid E) = I(M, \Sigma;\, E)
1056→\end{align*}
1057→where the last step uses $(M,\Sigma) \perp\!\!\!\perp S \mid E$ (which follows from $(M,\Sigma) \perp\!\!\!\perp S$ and the fact that conditioning on $E$, a function of $(M,\Sigma,S)$, cannot create dependence between independent variables). Therefore $I(X;E) - I(S;E) = I(M,\Sigma;\,E)$.
1058→
1059→\emph{Part (iii)}: If $E \perp\!\!\!\perp S \mid (M, \Sigma)$, then $E$ is determined entirely by $(M,\Sigma)$: $I(S;\,E) = I(S;\,E \mid M,\Sigma) + I(S;\,E;\,M,\Sigma) - \ldots$. More directly, $I(S;E) = 0$ because $S \to (M,\Sigma) \to E$ forms a Markov chain under this assumption. Therefore $I(X;E) - I(S;E) = I(X;E) = I(M,\Sigma;E) = H(E) - H(E \mid M,\Sigma) = H(E)$ since $(M,\Sigma)$ determine $E$.
1060→\end{proof}
1061→
1062→\textbf{Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:hierarchy}} (Strict Head-Level Hierarchy: $\mathcal{C}_\text{head} \subset \mathcal{G}_\text{tmpl} \subset \mathcal{G}_\text{code}$).
1063→
1064→\begin{proof}
1065→\emph{$\mathcal{C}_\text{head} \subseteq \mathcal{G}_\text{tmpl}$}: Each $c \in \mathcal{C}_\text{head}$ is, by Definition~\ref{def:discrete_head}, a pooling strategy followed by an MLP head. Both components are expressible as template derivations of the form $S \to \text{Pool}\cdot\text{MLP}\cdot\text{Output}$ (Appendix~\ref{app:grammar}), so $c$ lies in $\mathcal{G}_\text{tmpl}$. \emph{Strict}: $\mathcal{G}_\text{tmpl}$ additionally admits Conv1d pooling and SiLU activations absent from $\mathcal{C}_\text{head}$.
1066→
1067→\emph{$\mathcal{G}_\text{tmpl} \subseteq \mathcal{G}_\text{code}$}: Every template derivation produces a valid Python program satisfying Definition~\ref{def:code}. \emph{Strict}: $\mathcal{G}_\text{code}$ contains depthwise convolutions, batch normalization, residual connections, and learned positional weights---none derivable from the template grammar (Table~\ref{tab:expressiveness}).
1068→
1069→\emph{Orthogonality of LoRA and bottleneck configurations.} Configurations in $\mathcal{C}\setminus\mathcal{C}_\text{head}$ modify the backbone's attention projections (LoRA) or inject residual modules between encoder blocks (bottleneck). They are not head-level adapters and are therefore not members of $\mathcal{G}_\text{tmpl}$ nor of $\mathcal{G}_\text{code}$ as defined (the code space is restricted to adapter classes operating on extracted hidden states, per Definition~\ref{def:code}). We compare against the LoRA variant separately under the strictly frozen backbone protocol in Table~\ref{tab:baselines}.
1070→\end{proof}
1071→
1072→\textbf{Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen}} (Gradient Co-Adaptation Drives Entropy Collapse).
1073→
1074→\begin{proof}
1075→Consider a single input $\mathbf{x}$ with backbone $\mathbf{h} = A\mathbf{x}$, router $p = \sigma(\mathbf{w}^\top\mathbf{h})$, and loss $\ell = p\,\ell_1(\mathbf{h}) + (1-p)\,\ell_2(\mathbf{h})$.
1076→
1077→\emph{Part (i): Unfrozen backbone.} The gradient with respect to $A$ is:
1078→\begin{equation*}
1079→\frac{\partial\ell}{\partial A} = p\,\frac{\partial\ell_1}{\partial\mathbf{h}}\mathbf{x}^\top + (1{-}p)\,\frac{\partial\ell_2}{\partial\mathbf{h}}\mathbf{x}^\top + (\ell_1 - \ell_2)\,p(1{-}p)\,\mathbf{w}\mathbf{x}^\top
1080→\end{equation*}
1081→Under gradient descent $\dot{A} = -\eta\,\partial\ell/\partial A$, the induced change in expert~1's loss is:
1082→\begin{equation*}
1083→\dot{\ell}_1\big|_A = \left(\frac{\partial\ell_1}{\partial\mathbf{h}}\right)^\top\dot{A}\,\mathbf{x} \approx -\eta\,p\left\|\frac{\partial\ell_1}{\partial\mathbf{h}}\right\|^2\|\mathbf{x}\|^2
1084→\end{equation*}
1085→and analogously $\dot{\ell}_2\big|_A \approx -\eta\,(1{-}p)\left\|\frac{\partial\ell_2}{\partial\mathbf{h}}\right\|^2\|\mathbf{x}\|^2$. When $p > 1{-}p$ (expert~1 dominates), $\ell_1$ decreases faster than $\ell_2$. The third term in $\partial\ell/\partial A$ further reinforces this: when $\ell_1 < \ell_2$, the factor $(\ell_1 - \ell_2) < 0$ drives $\dot{p} > 0$, pushing $p$ toward~1. Together these create the self-reinforcing loop: dominant expert gets more gradient $\to$ its loss drops faster $\to$ router increases its weight $\to$ it becomes more dominant.
1086→
1087→\emph{Part (ii): Frozen backbone.} When $A$ is fixed, $\dot{A} = 0$. The only dynamics are through $\mathbf{w}$:
1088→\begin{equation*}
1089→\dot{\mathbf{w}} = -\eta\,\frac{\partial\ell}{\partial\mathbf{w}} = -\eta\,(\ell_1 - \ell_2)\,p(1{-}p)\,\mathbf{h}
1090→\end{equation*}
1091→This update adjusts routing weights based on current expert performance, but does \emph{not} reshape $\mathbf{h}$ to favor one expert. Without the $A$-mediated feedback loop, there is no systematic bias toward entropy collapse---the router converges to a stable allocation reflecting the static geometry of the fixed representation space.
1092→\end{proof}
1093→
1094→\textbf{Budget Sufficiency (Empirical).} Figure~\ref{fig:budget} shows that all 14 tested search budgets $B\in[20,480]$ beat the conv baseline on both ETTh1 and ETTm1 and that the MSE as a function of $B$ saturates around $B\!\approx\!60$. Evolutionary selection did not outperform random sampling in the template space $\mathcal{G}_\text{tmpl}$, indicating that the returns to additional samples past the saturation point are negligible for this space. We report this purely as an empirical observation from the curve; no theoretical sample-complexity bound is claimed.
1095→
1096→\section{AAS Search Details}
1097→\label{app:aas_details}
1098→
1099→The AAS evolutionary loop maintains a population of 20 adapters over 12 generations (${\sim}240$ total evaluations). Each evaluation performs a 3-epoch fitness probe ($\text{fitness} = -\text{MSE}_\text{val}$) on a single A10G GPU. Elitism preserves the top 2 architectures each generation; 18 new candidates are generated per generation. The population is initialized with 5 hand-designed seed adapters plus 15 from the template grammar. An LLM variant (gpt-4o-mini) generates candidates in the code space $\mathcal{G}_\text{code}$ using evolutionary context (parent code + error feedback). Template-based generation achieves 100\% validity (vs.\ ${\sim}75\%$ for LLM), enabling fair comparison that isolates search space effects from budget differences.

