1044→\begin{proof}
1045→\emph{Part (i)}: The RevIN map $N: \mathbf{x} \mapsto \mathbf{S} = (\mathbf{x} - M)/\Sigma$ is deterministic, so for any router that operates on $\mathbf{S}$ we have the Markov chain $X \to S \to E$. The Data Processing Inequality gives $I(X;E) \geq I(S;E)$.
1046→
1047→\emph{Part (ii)}: By the chain rule of mutual information,
1048→\begin{align*}
1049→I(X; E) &= I(M, \Sigma, S;\, E) = I(S;\, E) + I(M, \Sigma;\, E \mid S).
1050→\end{align*}
1051→Under the assumption $(M, \Sigma) \perp\!\!\!\perp S$, we expand:
1052→\begin{align*}
1053→I(M, \Sigma;\, E \mid S) &= H(M, \Sigma \mid S) - H(M, \Sigma \mid E, S) \\
1054→&= H(M, \Sigma) - H(M, \Sigma \mid E, S) = I(M, \Sigma;\, E, S) \\
1055→&= I(M, \Sigma;\, E) + \underbrace{I(M, \Sigma;\, S \mid E)}_{\geq\, 0}
1056→\end{align*}
1057→The non-negative residual $I(M,\Sigma; S \mid E)$ arises because $E$, as a function of $(M,\Sigma,S)$, acts as a collider: conditioning on $E$ can induce dependence between its otherwise independent parents $(M,\Sigma)$ and $S$ (Berkson's paradox). Therefore $I(X;E) - I(S;E) = I(M,\Sigma;\,E) + I(M,\Sigma;\,S \mid E) \geq I(M,\Sigma;\,E)$, with equality iff $(M,\Sigma) \perp\!\!\!\perp S \mid E$.
1058→
1059→\emph{Part (iii)}: If $E \perp\!\!\!\perp S \mid (M, \Sigma)$, then $E$ is determined entirely by $(M,\Sigma)$: $I(S;\,E) = I(S;\,E \mid M,\Sigma) + I(S;\,E;\,M,\Sigma) - \ldots$. More directly, $I(S;E) = 0$ because $S \to (M,\Sigma) \to E$ forms a Markov chain under this assumption. Therefore $I(X;E) - I(S;E) = I(X;E) = I(M,\Sigma;E) = H(E) - H(E \mid M,\Sigma) = H(E)$ since $(M,\Sigma)$ determine $E$.
